# FreeLinX patches for NetBSD sh (bin/sh)

This directory holds FreeLinX-specific patches applied on top of the pristine
NetBSD 10.1 source. It contains ONLY small, isolated, documented changes
required to build sh against the FreeLinX musl toolchain — never large
rewrites or giant hacks.

## Layout note

NetBSD 10.1 keeps `/bin/sh` at `bin/sh` (the classic `external/bsd/bin/sh`
path is from older releases). The port's `do-prepare` normalizes the extracted
`usr/src` tree to the NetBSD src root and symlinks `external/bsd/bin` ->
`../../bin`, so `external/bsd/bin/sh` and `bin/sh` are the same directory.

## Distinguishing sources (keep this straight)

  - UPSTREAM SOURCE    NetBSD 10.1 (fetched once into dist/; see ../distinfo).
                       Textually modified ONLY by these FreeLinX patches.
  - FREELINX PATCHES   Files in this directory (applied in lexical order by
                       the port's do-prepare).
  - FREELINX COMPAT    ../compat/: musl-libc stand-ins compiled into the link
                       set (see below); NOT part of the NetBSD tree.
  - BUILD FRAMEWORK    This repo (mk/, scripts/, config/). Not upstream.
  - GENERATED ARTIFACTS build/, staging/, dist/. Ignored by git.

## musl compatibility strategy

Two complementary mechanisms, both small and deliberately scoped:

  1. ../compat/sys/cdefs.h is the FreeLinX stand-in for BSD `<sys/cdefs.h>`
     (musl has none). Because every sh source includes it first, it also
     carries the small libc gaps the NetBSD code expects from BSD
     `<sys/param.h>`/`<sys/types.h>` (BSD, ptrdiff_t/stdint types, timespeccmp)
     plus the declarations for the shims in ../compat.
  2. ../compat/*.c are compiled into the static link set:
       getprogname.c  getprogname/setprogname/setproctitle (musl: absent)
       setmode.c      setmode(3)/getmode(3) for `umask`'s symbolic mode —
                      NetBSD's own libc source, minus namespace.h, with
                      reallocarr->reallocarray and S_ISTXT->S_ISVTX.
       signalname.c   signalname/signalnumber/signalnext for `trap` and
                      `kill -l`.  Deliberately keyed to musl's <signal.h>
                      macros: BSD and Linux signal NUMBERING differ (e.g.
                      SIGCONT is 19 on BSD but 18 on Linux), so a verbatim
                      NetBSD table would be wrong on FreeLinX.
  3. Build-time host helpers: ../compat/netbsd-mktemp emulates NetBSD
     `mktemp -t` for NetBSD's own source generators (mkoptions.sh).  Host
     awk/sed/sh run those generators exactly as NetBSD's USETOOLS cross-build
     does; they produce C, they are not the delivered binary.

## Patch inventory (apply in this lexical order)

  - patch-myhistedit-nohistory
        Build without history/editline: guard the <histedit.h>/<filecomplete.h>
        includes under #ifndef SMALL.  histedit.c is still compiled; under
        -DSMALL its #else stubs provide histcmd()/inputrc().
  - patch-main-fmaxfd-linux
        musl has no fcntl(F_MAXFD); derive the fd ceiling from
        sysconf(_SC_OPEN_MAX) instead.
  - patch-cd-getcwd-linux
        On Linux, use the libc getcwd() loop instead of forking /bin/pwd.
  - patch-unused-attr-musl
        Drop the NetBSD __unused macro markers.  musl's <sys/stat.h> uses
        `long __unused[3]` as a literal member name, so __unused must NOT be a
        macro at all; the two sh sites are patched to not rely on it.
  - patch-setjmp-musl
        error.h remaps setjmp->_setjmp (BSD signal-mask semantics).  musl has
        no _setjmp, and its setjmp is already the plain (non-mask) kind, so
        skip the remap on __linux__.
  - patch-eval-syspath-musl
        syspath() read the system PATH via sysctl(CTL_USER/USER_CS_PATH), an
        interface musl lacks.  FreeLinX is a standalone distro with no
        "system" path, so return the conventional default.

## Rules of engagement for new patches

Is it POSIX? -> does musl provide it? -> does sh actually need it? -> only then
add a minimal patch, and document why right here in the next bullet.
